WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
"Benefits of Blueberries for Blood
Pressure May Be Blocked by Yogurt"

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.620
A trio of Harvard studies that
followed more than 100,000 women

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.940
for more than a decade found
that those consuming

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.230
the most anthocyanins (the
brightly colored pigments found

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.381
in berries, like blueberries
and strawberries)

00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:22.440
had an 8% reduction in risk of
developing high blood pressure,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.960
And the group consuming the
most every day were only eating

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.700
about six strawberries worth or even
just 11 blueberries, a tenth of a cup.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.022
But maybe the biggest berry
eaters just happened to have

00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:37.550
other healthy habits and that's
the real reason they did better.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.620
After all, you're probably more likely
to sprinkle blueberries on oatmeal

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.109
than bacon and eggs.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:46.110
But they controlled for whole
grain intake and fiber and salt

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.710
and smoking and exercise
and a bunch of other things,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.600
and the berry benefit still remained.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.910
But you don't know for sure
until you put it to the test.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.589
A randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled clinical trial, and—

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:03.320
the title gives away
the thrilling conclusion:

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.560
"Daily Blueberry Consumption
Improves Blood Pressure"

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.380
How can you do a double-blind
trial, though, with a food?

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.410
How can you convincingly create
a fake placebo blueberry?

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.490
They used whole blueberries—
about a cup's worth—

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.860
but powdered them versus
a lookalike placebo powder,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.660
which had the same amount of sugar
and calories as the real blueberries,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.000
but without actual blueberries.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.800
Those in the placebo control group:
no real change over the 8-week study.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.050
They started out 138 over 79,
and ended up 139 over 80,

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:40.500
whereas the real blueberry group
fell from 138 over 80

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.010
to 131 over 75, a significant drop.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.180
Now, 131 is still too high.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.070
You'd like to see at least 
down to 120 or even 110,

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.120
so blueberries alone may not cure you.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.820
However, the fact that you could get
a clinically significant improvement

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.110
in a killer disease by just adding

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.920
a single thing to your diet,
that's pretty impressive.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.890
Is more better?

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.550
What about twice the dose, more like
2 cups of fresh blueberries a day?

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.120
Same kind of significant drop,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.780
but didn't seem to work any better,
so one cup may do it.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.100
Even less may work.
It's never been tested.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.450
Overall, there's been five
interventional studies to date

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.050
on the effects of blueberry
supplementation on blood pressure.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.230
Put all the studies together

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:29.340
and the results do not
show any clinical efficacy.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.590
Wait, wait, what?

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:37.750
I just showed you two studies where
there was this gorgeous effect.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.670
Have I been cherry picking studies,
or rather berry picking studies?

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:47.330
Well, if you look closely at the studies,
the blueberries in the two studies

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.590
I showed you that detected a significant
effect were prepared with water;

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.410
they just mixed the blueberry
powder with water.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.790
However, the blueberries in
the non-significant effect studies

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.400
were prepared with yogurt or
skim milk-based smoothies.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.209
If you remember my blast-from-the-past
video from like 8 years ago,

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:09.090
the absorption of berry nutrients
can be blocked by dairy.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.090
Mix strawberries with water

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.400
and you get a nice peak
in strawberry phytonutrients

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.380
in your bloodstream within
hours of consumption.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.069
But if you instead go for
strawberries with cream—

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.080
mixing the same amount of
strawberries with milk instead—

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.750
significantly less makes
it into your system.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.930
The inhibitory effects of milk are
thought to be due to the interaction

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.230
between the berry pigments
and milk proteins.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:33.489
Yeah, but does the same
thing happen with blueberries?

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:34.819
Let's find out.

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:38.230
Hard to maintain the suspense
when the title just gives it away.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:43.800
But indeed, the antioxidant activity
of blueberries is impaired by milk.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.760
Volunteers ate a cup and a half of
blueberries with water or with milk,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.780
and the milk blocked the absorption
of some phytonutrients, but not others.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.220
So, did it really matter
that much though?

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.240
Here's the spikes in the bloodstream
after blueberries with water,

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.270
and here's how much is
absorbed with milk.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.720
OK, so less, but check out
what happened

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.820
to the total antioxidant capacity
of your bloodstream.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.350
Eat blueberries alone, with water,

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.400
and the antioxidant power
of your bloodstream

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.839
shoots up within an hour and
remains elevated 5 hours later.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:25.950
OK, so with milk, you'd be thinking
there'd maybe less of a bump, right?

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.520
You can say that again; not just less,
but less than when you started from.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.010
You just ate a whole bowl of
blueberries and ended up with less

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:38.470
antioxidant capacity in your body
because you ate them with milk.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.750
No wonder mixing blueberries
with yogurt or milk

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.090
may abolish the blood-pressure
lowering benefits.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:49.380
Interestingly, full-fat milk may inhibit
nutrient absorption the most,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.550
similar to what one finds adding milk
to tea: twice the reduction in in-vitro

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.970
antioxidant values with whole
milk, compared to skim milk,

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.030
which is weird because
we always thought

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.000
it was the milk protein
that was the culprit.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.090
This suggests there may be some

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:10.000
nutrient-blocking involvement
from the dairy fat as well.

